{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":27477807,"ns":0,"title":"Constant conjunction","extract":"In philosophy, constant conjunction is a relationship between two events, where one event is invariably followed by the other: if the occurrence of A is always followed by B, A and B are said to be constantly conjoined. A critical philosophical question concerns the relationship between constant conjunction and causation, which has implications in the philosophy of science.","description":"Phrase used in philosophy as a variant or near synonym for causality and induction","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}